over fixed-width integers is made.
Otherwise, the results would be inflated by e.g. 107M files using \code("py"){int} in Python.
}

\:::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::

\h2{Motivation}

\h3{Use cases}

In terms of utility,
\tcode{std::big_int} use cases can be classified into two buckets:
\ol{
  \li{
    The use case where integers are \dfn{potentially big}.
    That is, the numbers involved are typically still small,
    but since no fixed-width integers are used,
    they might sometimes be big.
  }
  \li{
    The use case where integers are \dfn{actually big}.
  }
}

\tcode{std::big_int} has many use cases,
falling into one of those buckets:

\style{
#use-cases-table td {
  border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color);
}
}

\table(id="use-cases-table"){
  \tr{
    \th{Use case}
    \th{Goal}
    \th{Bucket}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Serialization}
    \td{
      JSON, YAML, etc. support integers without any upper bound set by the markup format,
      and large integers need to be represented somehow.
    }
    \td(rowspan="3"){Potentially big}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Safety}
    \td{
      Avoiding UB or correctness issues from integer overflow,
      at some runtime cost.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Scripting engines}
    \td{
      Scripting languages often support infinite-precision integers
      (e.g. Python's \tcode{int}),
      and their engines need to represent those.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Statistics/combinatorics}
    \td{
      Large factorials require much wider integers than 64-bit.
    }
    \td(rowspan="3"){Actually big}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Scientific computing}
    \td{
      Binomial coefficients,
      primality tests,
      integer relation algorithms,
      partition numbers,
      and many more
      may be computed with thousands of bits.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Cryptography}
    \td{
      Many cryptographic algorithms such as RSA require calculations with
      hundreds or thousands of bits.
    }
  }
}

\: NOTE: Make sure to keep this list in sync with #many-trade-offs

\h3{\tcode{std::big_int} is a vocabulary type}

An important reason why \tcode{std::big_int} should be in the C++ standard library
is that it is a vocabulary type and may appear at library boundaries.

\Bex{
A single code base may depend on different libraries that all interact with \tcode{std::big_int}:
\ol{
  \li{
    A library that loads config files (JSON, YAML, etc.)
    can return deserialized integers as \tcode{std::big_int}.
  }
  \li{
    Those \tcode{std::big_int} objects are then processed by a numeric library
    that uses them in e.g. statistics.
  }
  \li{
    The results are then stored by another library that serializes \tcode{std::big_int}
    to e.g. CSV.
    Alternatively, the results are stored in a relational database,
    using a C++ API that uses \tcode{std::big_int} for big integer columns in a table.
  }
}
}

\Bex{
In Java,
a variety of cryptographic vocabulary types are built on top of \tcode{BigInteger},
such as \tcode{RSAPublicKey} and \tcode{RSAPrivateKey}.
These higher-level vocabulary types are then used in various other parts of the
networking and cryptography stack,
like the TLS implementation, Java Key Stores, etc.

This is also a plausible use case in C++.
While RSA calculations may internally use fixed-width integers,
\tcode{std::big_int} simplifies top-level interfaces by \q{type-erasing} the width.
That is, the integer type is the same,
regardless of the RSA key size or other constants.
}

\Bex{
Similar to Java's \tcode{BigDecimal},
an infinite-precision decimal floating-point type can be formed as follows:
\cppblock{
struct big_decimal {
  std::big_int unscaled;
  int scale; // exponent for multiplication with base 10
};
}
The value represented here is \tcode{(unscaled * pow(10, scale))}.

Such a type would appear at a library boundary for communicating with a PostgreSQL database,
which supports \tcode{decimal} and \tcode{numeric} among its
\ref("https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-numeric.html"){Numeric Types}.
Note that PostgreSQL supports potentially huge decimal types,
like \q{up to 131072 before the decimal point},
so even with C23 types like \tcode{_Decimal128},
those could not be represented.
These types map onto the \tcode{java.math.BigDecimal} type in Java libraries.
}

\Bex{
Almost all relational databases support some kind of infinite-precision numeric type
(subject to implementation limits),
be that \q{big integer} or \q{big decimal}.
The workaround for C's and C++'s lack of infinite-precision types in libpq
(the PostgreSQL C library)
is to either
\ul{
  \li{
    represent integers and decimal numbers as strings, or
  }
  \li{
    convert to libpq's binary numeric format
    (and the conversion from e.g. \tcode{boost::cpp_int} to this format is fairly complicated).
  }
}
In Java, such interfaces are obvious and ergonomic by comparison,
operating on \tcode{java.math.BigInteger} or \tcode{java.math.BigDecimal}.
This also makes it possible to build higher-level database abstractions
like the \ref("https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/technotes/guides/jdbc/"){JDBC API}
on top of concrete \q{database drivers}
(for Microsoft SQL, PostgreSQL, MySQL, H2, etc.)
without converting big numeric types at each layer.

By comparison, if someone wanted to create a high-level, generic API for databases in C++,
they would not have a common vocabulary type for big integers that can be passed through
the various API layers unchanged.
\tcode{std::big_int} is that crucial missing type.
}

\Bex{
Symbolic math libraries typically create their own \q{big integer} class,
possibly as a wrapper around gmp.
For example, there is
\ref("https://symengine.org/symengine/classSymEngine_1_1Integer.html"){SymEngine::Integer},
\ref("https://www.ginac.de/reference/classGiNaC_1_1numeric.html"){GiNaC::numeric}, etc.
This is also typically the case for scripting language engines if the language
has a big integer type (like Python or JavaScript).
This ultimately means that a single application can end up with several \q{big integer}
implementations at the same time to implement those wrappers:
some \q{manual},
some using a statically linked \tt{gmp},
some using Boost.Multiprecision,
etc.

\tcode{std::big_int} can solve that problem by turning all these fragmented wrappers
into wrappers around a single standard type,
or by removing the wrappers altogether.
Libraries can simply use \tcode{std::big_int} directly
just like they use \tcode{std::string} directly.
}

\Bex{
GUI toolkits in C++ typically don't bother with direct support for big integers,
even though the runtime cost of using such integers
compared to rendering the GUI is negligible.
For example,
GTK's \ref("https://docs.gtk.org/gtk3/class.SpinButton.html"){\tt{SpinButton}}
takes \tcode{double} inputs and
Qt's \ref("https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qspinbox.html"){\tt{QSpinBox}}
uses \tcode{int}.

Such limitations are arbitrary and unnecessary when infinite-precision numeric types
are available for use in GUIs.
While the internal editing state of a widget needs to be represented as text
(to represent partial inputs like \q{\tt{123.}} before the user types the fractional part),
input and output of a \tcode{big_int} or \tcode{big_decimal} value is clearly useful
for number fields.
}

In some sense,
\tcode{std::big_int} is an even more important vocabulary type
than \tcode{std::vector} and \tcode{std::string}
because there is no convenient fallback solution.
If those containers were not available,
users could still communicate at library boundaries by using pointers and sizes.
Strings can be passed to other libraries as a \tcode{char*} (and maybe \tcode{size_t}),
so while \tcode{std::string} is an important container type
and helps with managing allocation ownership,
it is arguably gratuitous as a vocabulary type.
Similarly, if \tcode{std::span} and \tcode{std::string_view} were not available,
they could easily be replaced with the pointer and the size that they wrap.

By comparison to contiguous containers and views,
there is no widely established convention for passing large integers between libraries in C++,
other than using their string representation,
which comes at significant cost.
Consequently, we see a fragmented ecosystem in C++,
where every logging, database, cryptography, and serialization library
has its own solution to passing big integers and often its own types.
\tcode{std::big_int} could lay the foundation for passing big integers between libraries
just as easily as in other languages.

\Bnote{
Even though \tcode{std::big_int} is not directly C-compatible,
one could pair a \tcode{uint_multiprecision_t*} and \tcode{size_t},
where the passed \q{limb array} has the same layout as \tcode{std::big_int::representation}.
This means that such a C++ type might establish a valuable convention
for passing big integers in C libraries,
even if not directly available.
}

\h3{\tcode{std::big_int} for convenience and correctness}

Another reason why \tcode{std::big_int} needs to be in the standard library
is that users would often avoid it otherwise,
even when it is the right tool for the job.
For example, it could be quite plausible that a library has to handle integers
with more than 64 bits in some edge cases,
but if the library author needs to add a dependency on the whole of Boost.Multiprecision
to replace 2-3 lines of code using \tcode{std::intmax_t} with \tcode{boost::cpp_int},
they may just not do it.

C++ developers don't get a \em{fair} choice between
a fixed-width type for performance and an infinite-precision type for correctness;
they get to choose between readily available fixed-width types
and adding a heavy dependency like GMP or Boost.Multiprecision for correctness,
which they may not do,
\em{especially} if that dependency is transitively forced onto their library users.

\h3{\tcode{std::big_int} is platform-dependent and hard to implement}

Another reason why \tcode{std::big_int} needs to be in the standard library
is that it's extremely difficult to implement and optimize,
in part because the implementation depends heavily on the platform's hardware capabilities,
many of which are not exposed portably in the language.

\Bex{
Some examples of platform and compiler-specific features:
\ul{
  \li{
    x86_64 has a \tt{mul} instruction that yields
    both the low and the high bits of a multiplication;
    without utilizing that,
    multiprecision multiplication is much slower.
  }
  \li{
    x86_64 also has a \tt{div} instruction that takes a 128-bit dividend and a 64-bit divisor,
    which can be used to implement division of an arbitrary-length dividend much more efficiently.
  }
  \li{
    Funnel-shifting (basically a 128-bit shift)
    is a common operation in multiprecision arithmetic,
    and Clang recently got a \tcode{__builtin_elementwise_funnel_shl} intrinsics that exposes
    the hardware instruction.
  }
  \li{
    Bitwise operations between \tcode{std::big_int}
    can be performed in parallel using SIMD intrinsics.
    While those can be portably implemented using \header{simd} now,
    that may add unnecessary overhead compared to using the underlying intrinsics directly.
  }
  \li{
    Conversions between \tcode{std::big_int} and floating-point types may be
    hardware-accelerated or backed by special routines in the runtime library.
    For example, to convert \tcode{float} to \tcode{std::big_int},
    the conversion should go through \tcode{__fixunssfti}
    (\tcode{float} \'RIGHTWARDS ARROW' \tcode{unsigned __int128})
    followed by a conversion to \tcode{std::big_int}.
    Note that an unsigned 128-bit integer is sufficient to represent any finite
    binary32 floating-point number without the fractional part.
  }
  \li{
    During constant evaluation,
    if \tcode{_BitInt} is available,
    it is often faster to implement an operation in terms of \tcode{_BitInt}.
    For example, a 4096-bit division can be delegated entirely to compiler intrinsics
    by converting the dividend and divisor to \tcode{_BitInt(4096)},
    performing the division in terms of e.g. \tcode{llvm::APInt} operations.
    This completely avoids the need for constant evaluation of complex division routines.
  }
}
}

In general, the intrinsics necessary to implement \tcode{std::big_int} efficiently
vary wildly from compiler to compiler
and from architecture to architecture.
It also varies a lot whether those intrinsics can be used during constant evaluation,
and these implementation details are subject to frequent change and added features.
Our \ref("Reference-Impl") of \tcode{std::big_int} is a sea of \tcode{if constexpr}
and \tcode{#ifdef} checks.

Overall, it is practically impossible or at least extremely difficult for a third-party library
to keep up with all these details to implement the type optimally,
across all compilers and all supported architectures.
Features like these should ideally live directly in the compiler or in the standard library.

\h3(
  id="big-int-compiler-intrinsic",
){\tt{std::big_int} should be a compiler intrinsic for constant evaluation}

While a portable implementation of \tcode{std::big_int} is possible,
an ideal implementation for constant evaluation simply delegates
to the compiler's internal infinite-precision integer type (e.g. \tcode{llvm::APInt} in LLVM).
This is because constant evaluation is relatively expensive
compared to highly optimized multiprecision code in the compiler internals.
A third-party library such as Boost.Multiprecision
does not have the luxury of adding new compiler intrinsics,
so the implementation quality could never match the potential of \tcode{std::big_int}.

A closely related issue is the implementation of the user-defined literal,
which has the following interface in our proposal:
\cppblock{
template<char... digits> constexpr big_int operator""n();
template<char... digits> constexpr big_int operator""N();

// now possible:
big_int x = 123'456'789'012'345'678'901'234'567'890n;
}

The problem is that there is no other form of \tcode{operator""n}
that enables this syntax,
and the \tcode{char...} form requires manual parsing of the digits during constant evaluation,
rather than delegating this to the compiler.
It would be much better if parsing could be done in the compiler
(and it already does actually)
to provide a form such as:
\cppblock{
constexpr big_int operator""n(const uint_multiprecision_t* limbs, size_t size);
}

With this form,
\ul{
  \li{digit separators would be skipped automatically,}
  \li{base prefixes like \tcode{0x} would be handled by the compiler, and}
  \li{forming the limb array would be done by the compiler, which is the expensive part,}
  \li{template instantiations would be avoided completely.}
}

That being said,
this new form of \tcode{operator""n"} is not part of this proposal,
but it does demonstrate why some compiler support is needed.

\:::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::

\h2{Design}

\h3{Design strategy}

The most important anchor points of the design are:

\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{std::big_int} should be elastic;
    that is, it should grow to the necessary size required to fit the result
    of an arithmetic operation.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::big_int} should support custom allocators.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::big_int} should expose \q{small object optimization} to the user.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::big_int} should grant access to the underlying representation.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::big_int} operations should be \tcode{constexpr}.
  }
}

This culminates in the following library declarations:

\cppblock{
// Class template:
template<size_t min_inplace_capacity,
         class Limb = uint_multiprecision_t,
         class Allocator = allocator<uint_multiprecision_t>>
  class basic_big_int;

// Alias with default in-place capacity and default allocator
using big_int = basic_big_int<\exposid{implementation-defined}>;
}

Conceptually, a \tcode{basic_big_int} holds:
\cppblock{
union {
  // In-place representation, used for small values:
  uint_multiprecision_t inplace_storage[N];
  // Dynamic representation, used for large values:
  struct {
    uint_multiprecision_t* dynamic_storage;
    size_t dynamic_capacity;
  };
};
}

The underlying arithmetic is then implemented to operate on \dfn{limb arrays},
i.e. arrays of \tcode{uint_multiprecision_t} objects,
agnostic of whether the integer value is stored in-place or dynamically.

\h3{Layout}

\h4{\tt{std::uint_multiprecision_t}}

A user-facing integer type alias introduced by this paper is \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t},
which is an unsigned integer type with the following important properties:
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t} has no padding bits.
    Padding bits in a limb type would be wasteful and make things more difficult both
    for users and for implementations.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t} is the widest type that has arithmetic hardware support.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t} has no explicit minimum width,
    but effectively cannot be narrower than \tcode{unsigned char} due to being unpadded.
  }
}

In summary, it is the unsigned integer type most suitable for multiprecision.
Colloquially it is the largest unsigned integer which has arithmetic instructions.

Curiously, there is no existing type with the desired properties
despite the abundance of type aliases in \header{cstdint} and \header{cstddef}:
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{std::uint_fast32_t} is at least 32 bits wide,
    which means that on 16-bit architectures,
    the multiprecision code would have two layers:
    the emulation of 32-bit integers on 16-bit hardware,
    and then the multiprecision of \tcode{std::big_int} operations.
    This is highly questionable because the point of \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t}
    is to have a type whose operations correspond directly to hardware instructions.
    Also, \tcode{std::uint_fast32_t} is not consistently 64 bits wide on targets
    with 64-bit arithmetic support.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::size_t} often could be used as \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t},
    but on targets like WASM32,
    it is only 32 bits wide despite WASM32 having 64-bit arithmetic instructions for \tt{i64}.
    It would be extremely wasteful to only use 32-bit operations when 64 bits are available.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::uintptr_t} typically has the same problem as \tcode{std::size_t}.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{unsigned long} is only 32 bits wide on 64-bit Windows.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{unsigned long long} is always 64 bits wide,
    even on 32-bit targets.
  }
}

\h4{C compatibility}

While \tcode{std::big_int} is not \em{really} C-compatible by virtue of being a class template
full of C++ features,
it is still possible to provide a C-compatible interface
taking \tcode{uint_multiprecision_t[]} and implementing its functionality in C++.
This relies on the fact that the internal layout of \tcode{std::big_int} is specified in detail.

\Bex{
One could write a header that is C-interoperable and implemented in C++:
\cppblock{
// header.h
#ifdef __cplusplus
using std::uint_multiprecision_t;
#else
typedef /* ... */ uint_multiprecision_t;
#endif

#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C"
#endif
void do_math(const uint_multiprecision_t* data, size_t size);
}
\cppblock{
// source.cpp
#include "header.h"
extern "C" void do_math(const uint_multiprecision_t* data, size_t size) {
  std::big_int x{std::from_range, std::span{data, size}};
  // do math with x ...
}
}

This is analogous to having a C header taking \tcode{const char*}
and implementing the function internally using \tcode{std::string} in C++.
}

However, this somewhat assumes that \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t}
also becomes available in C,
possibly in \header{cstdint} / \header{stdint.h}.
Not having the type in C means that C users would need to recreate the type choice
the implementation makes or use implementation-specific definitions like a
predefined \tcode{__UINT_MULTIPRECISION_T__} macro.

C compatibility ambitions are not in scope for the paper right now,
but are worth exploring in the future.

\h4{Small object optimizations}

\tcode{std::big_int} (or generally, infinite-precision integer types)
benefit from \dfn{small object optimization}
perhaps more than any other vocabulary type.
This is because with a relatively small amount of in-place storage
(e.g. 8 bytes for 64-bit numbers),
a lot of applications would \em{never} need to allocate dynamic storage to hold the value,
or only for extreme and unusual program inputs.
Furthermore,
once the numbers are huge enough to require dynamic allocation,
the cost of multiprecision arithmetic often hugely outweighs
the small amount of overhead caused by having a few unused bytes of in-place storage.

\Bnote{
By comparison,
all major standard libraries have small object optimization for \tcode{std::string},
but many strings exceed the in-place capacity (around 20 bytes),
so dynamic allocation for fairly short strings remains common.
}

The optimization is also necessary to prevent excessive allocations for small intermediate results.
For example, even for huge inputs \tcode{x} and \tcode{y},
the difference \tcode{x - y}, the quotient \tcode{x / y},
or the remainder \tcode{x % y} could still be fairly small numbers (possibly zero),
and it is wasteful to dynamically allocate for tiny integers.

\Bnote{
Concretely, in our benchmark results,
a single-limb \tcode{std::big_int} using SOO performs operations like addition
4×–10× faster than without SOO.\br
See \url{https://eisenwave.github.io/std-big-int/benchmarks.html#benchmarks_small_value}.
}

Because performing this optimization for \tcode{std::big_int} is such a no-brainer
and any reasonable implementation should have it,
we propose to expose it to the user via the \tcode{min_inplace_capacity} template parameter.
This parameter specifies the minimum integer width that \tcode{std::basic_big_int}
must be able to represent without dynamic allocation.
While \tcode{std::big_int} should be used in most cases and provides a reasonable default,
there may be scenarios where
\ul{
  \li{
    on 32-bit platforms, the capacity could be lowered to \tcode{32} or raised to \tcode{64}
    to prefer performance or correctness,
  }
  \li{
    on platforms where allocations are particularly expensive
    or where dynamic storage is very limited,
    the capacity could be raised to \tcode{128} or even \tcode{256}.
  }
}

\Bnote{
In our \ref("Reference-Impl"),
\tcode{std::big_int::inplace_capacity} is always 64 regardless of architecture.
On 64-bit, our layout is as follows:
\cppblock{
// Most significant bit stores the sign;
// the lower 31 bits store the limb count.
uint32_t size_and_sign;
// The capacity of the dynamic storage in limbs.
// If zero, indicates that there is no allocation and that inplace_storage is active.
uint32_t capacity;
union {
  // For values up to 64 bits.
  // For big_int, N = 1, but the min_inplace_capacity parameter allows more capacity
  // for other basic_big_int specializations.
  uint64_t inplace_storage[N];
  // For anything that doesn't fit into inplace_storage.
  uint64_t* dynamic_storage;
};
}

This layout ensures that \tcode{std::big_int}
\ul{
  \li{is two pointers large,}
  \li{is aligned to one pointer,}
  \li{is nothrow-movable and trivially relocatable,}
  \li{can represent both \tcode{int64_t} and \tcode{uint64_t} values without allocating, and}
  \li{
    can represent values with up to \math{\msup{\mn{2}\mrow{\mn{31}\mo{+}\mn{6}}}} bits
    (137 billion bits).
  }
}
}

\h4{Sign and magnitude}

\tcode{std::big_int} uses a sign-and-magnitude representation
(as opposed to e.g. two's complement),
and this is exposed to the user.
While it may seem like constraining implementation freedom unnecessarily,
the chosen representation impacts the complexity requirements of various operations.
For example, if the sign bit is separately stored,
then negation can be implemented in constant time by just flipping the sign bit,
whereas for two's complement,
it requires linear time because bits of the magnitude need updating.

Most implementations of infinite-precision integers use sign-and-magnitude representation,
as does our \ref("Reference-Impl").
This is not only due to those beneficial complexity requirements,
but also because it simplifies the implementation greatly.
For example:
multiplicative operators like multiplication and division can be implemented
solely in terms of the magnitude,
and the sign bit of the result can be computed separately.

\h5{Bitwise operations}

The only major downside of sign-and-magnitude representation is that it requires
emulation of two's complement for bitwise operations,
but this is relatively cheap and does not require an additional pass over the data.

\Bex{
Consider how to compute \tcode{a & b} with negative inputs
in such a way that is consistent with the two's complement behavior.
To do this in a single pass, for each limb, we need to
\ol{
  \li{negate each of the operand limbs if they belong to a negative \tcode{std::big_int},}
  \li{perform a bitwise AND, and}
  \li{negate the resulting limb if the sign bit of the result is negative.}
}
}

\Bnote{
\tcode{-x} is equivalent to \tcode{(~x + 1)},
so these \q{on the fly negations} are done by performing a bitwise NOT and interleaving
the whole operation with an add-with-carry.

In bitwise operations,
negative inputs are treated as if they were preceded by an infinite sequence of leading ones, and
positive inputs are treated as if they were preceded by an infinite sequence of leading zeros.
The sign bit of the result can be computed before any of the result limbs
by performing the bitwise operation on the leading \q{infinite bit value} of the operands.
}

\h4{Access to the underlying representation}

As mentioned in \ref("#design"),
a \tcode{std::big_int} is conceptually a \tcode{union} of
\tcode{uint_multiprecision_t inplace_storage[N];} and
\tcode{uint_multiprecision_t* dynamic_storage;}.
This storage should be directly accessible using a function.
Currently, we facilitate this using:

\cppblock{
constexpr span<const uint_multiprecision_t> representation() const noexcept;
}

The obvious downside of giving the user such direct access is that it breaks encapsulation,
and it constrains the \q{small object type} that the implementation could use.
For example, it isn't possible to have an \tcode{__int128 inplace_storage}
because \tcode{uint_multiprecision_t} cannot alias \tcode{__int128}.

Despite those downsides,
giving the user low-level access to the storage is necessary because it enables the user
to provide additional operations on \tcode{std::big_int} without overhead.

\Bex{
Consider how a user would implement their own \tcode{std::popcount} function
(counting the number of one-bits within the integer):
\cppblock{
int popcount(const std::big_int& x) noexcept {
  int sum = 0;
  for (std::uint_multiprecision_t d : x.representation()) {
    sum += std::popcount(d);
  }
  return sum;
}
}
Without this kind of low-level access,
the user would have to repeatedly perform bitwise operations such as \tcode{>>} and \tcode{&}
in order to perform the operation limb-by-limb.

Furthermore, it is unclear how the operation could be performed without allocations.
Even if the result of \tcode{(x >> N) & M} fits into a 64-bit integer and needs no allocation,
any intermediate result of \tcode{(x >> N)} may require allocation.
}

\Bnote{
Boost.Multiprecision provides access to the underlying representation
via a \tcode{limbs()} member function.
}

Crucially, we only provide read access to the representation, not write access.
Providing write access would make it easily possible to put the \tcode{std::big_int}
into a corrupt state, like
having a \tcode{size()} that doesn't match the number of bits in the representation, or
producing negative zero.

\h4{Why not use reference counting?}

An idea very early in the design process was to use reference counting in order to avoid
the issue explained in \ref("#optimal-operator-overloads").
That is, if \tcode{std::big_int} was essentially a
\tcode{shared_ptr<uint_multiprecision_t[]>},
it would be possible to pass it cheaply by value.

\Bex{
A function signature as follows would not be problematic:
\cppblock{
std::big_int abs(std::big_int x) {
  return x < 0 ? -std::move(x) : std::move(x);
}
}
If \tcode{x} has unique ownership (i.e. the reference count is 1),
\tcode{-x} can modify the integer in-place;
otherwise, \tcode{-x} performs a copy.
}

While this idea seems clever at first, there are several problems:
\ul{
  \li{
    The same was historically attempted for types like \tcode{std::string} to no success.
    Boost implementers gave negative feedback on this idea being pursued for
    \tcode{std::big_int}, and Boost.Multiprecision performs no reference counting either.
    A key problem is difficulty of implementation.
  }
  \li{
    Reference counting needs to be atomic because in the standard library,
    read-only access like copy constructors is generally thread-safe.
    Atomic operations can have significant overhead.
  }
  \li{
    Actually making use of the optimization opportunity in the example above
    (that is, modifying \tcode{std::big_int}'s data in-place if it has unique ownership)
    requires an atomic compare exchange (CAS):
    if the reference counter is currently one, set it to zero,
    otherwise other threads might believe they have unique ownership too.
    That also makes the \tcode{std::move} in the example necessary;
    we need permission to mess with the original object.
    Even with no contention,
    a CAS is a relatively expensive instruction.
  }
}

By comparison, a \tcode{std::big_int} design with unique ownership
avoids a lot of problems because it is statically known that it has unique ownership;
no runtime check is needed.

\h3{\tt{constexpr} support}

\tcode{std::big_int} also provides \tcode{constexpr} operations,
similar to \tcode{std::string}.

\Bnote{
Being able to perform multiprecision arithmetic at compile time is actually
part of the motivation for the paper.
\ref("#big-int-compiler-intrinsic") goes into more detail.
}

Without any dedicated compiler intrinsics,
this means that \tcode{std::big_int} manages an allocation,
similar to \tcode{std::string}.
That allocation is transient, i.e. it cannot persist to runtime.
However, performing operations on a \tcode{std::big_int} and freeing all memory
before the end of the constant expression is fine.

There is another layer to the design here,
which is that \tcode{std::big_int} is \em{mandated} to perform small object optimization,
and this is exposed to the user in the public API,
rather than just being an optional optimization like for \tcode{std::string}.
Because of this, we can have \tcode{std::big_int} values persist to runtime
as long as the value is small enough to require no allocation.

\Bex{
\cppblock{
// error: allocation not freed before the end of the constant expression
constexpr basic_big_int<64> x = static_cast<unsigned _BitInt(1024)>(-1);

// OK, -1 is representable within the object, and 64 bits are guaranteed
constexpr basic_big_int<64> x = -1;
}
}

\h4{Circumventing transient allocations}

One additional restriction on implementations to make this behavior reliable
is that they must never represent a \tcode{basic_big_int} dynamically
when inplace storage is sufficient.
At runtime, implementations might hold onto an allocation until \tcode{shrink_to_fit}
is called because that allocation may be immediately useful in the next operation.

\Bex{
We might do:
\cppblock{
basic_big_int<64> x = /* ... */; // pre-existing value which holds an allocation
basic_big_int<64> y = (std::move(x) - 1000) << 5;
}
Even if subtracting \tcode{1000} would make the difference small enough to be represented
as a 64-bit integer,
we do not (and should not) require freeing the storage of \tcode{x} \em{at runtime}.
That allocation is immediately useful to represent the result of the bit-shift.
}

However, \em{at compile time},
we require that \em{every operation} behaves as if \tcode{shrink_to_fit()}
was called on the result,
so allocations are aggressively freed if at all possible.
This is necessary because the result of the subtraction in the example
might be stored in a \tcode{constexpr} variable,
and this would fail unnecessarily because of \tcode{constexpr} allocation restrictions.

If we didn't have that behavior,
\tcode{constexpr std::big_int} variables might \q{randomly} fail to compile
from the perspective of the user, depending on the compiler,
for no good reason.

\h3{Optimal operator overloads}

The design includes operator overloads that are \q{optimal},
which requires handling two cases:
\ul{
  \li{
    When an operand is an rvalue of type \tcode{basic_big_int},
    its allocation can be reused for the operation.
  }
  \li{
    When an operand is of integral type,
    no temporary \tcode{basic_big_int} is created.
    This is important because the type may be \tcode{__int128}, \tcode{_BitInt(1024)}, etc.
    and may not be representable as \tcode{basic_big_int} without an unnecessary allocation.
  }
}

\Bex{
Consequently, the signature of binary operators looks like:
\cppblock{
template<class L, class R> constexpr \exposid{common-big-int-type}<L, R> operator+(L&& x, R&& y);
}

Unary operators become overload sets, like:
\cppblock{
constexpr basic_big_int operator+() const&;
constexpr basic_big_int operator+() && noexcept;
}
}

Each such binary operator must handle eight distinct cases,
which the \ref("Reference-Impl") classifies as follows:
\Bquote{
\cppblock{
// Convenience macro that describes one of eight forms of binary operation
// that any binary operation for `big_int` can take.
enum struct binary_op_form : unsigned char {
    // Both sides are movable.
    // Typically, the operation is performed by mutating the integer
    // with the most capacity.
    move_move,
    // Only the left side is movable, and its allocation is reused.
    // The right side is also a `basic_big_int`, but not movable.
    move_copy,
    // Only the right side is movable, and its allocation is reused.
    // The left side is also a `basic_big_int`, but not movable.
    copy_move,
    // Neither side is movable, so a fresh `basic_big_int` is created.
    copy_copy,
    // The left side is movable, and its allocation is reused.
    // The right side is a fundamental integer (of any cvref qualification).
    move_int,
    // The right side is movable, and its allocation is reused.
    // The left side is a fundamental integer (of any cvref qualification).
    int_move,
    // A fresh `basic_big_int` is created because the left side is not movable.
    // The right side is a fundamental integer (of any cvref qualification).
    copy_int,
    // A fresh `basic_big_int` is created because the right side is not movable.
    // The left side is a fundamental integer (of any cvref qualification).
    int_copy,
};
}
}

The benefit of such a design is that vast amounts of user code
are sped up without any effort by the user, such as:
\cppblock{
x + (y * z);     // operator+ reuses allocation
x += y * z;      // operator+= reuses allocation
(x + y) / 2;     // operator/ reuses allocation
x - std::abs(y); // operator- reuses allocation
}
Since \tcode{(y * z)} returns \tcode{basic_big_int} by value,
\tcode{operator+} can repurpose the allocation of that result.
By comparison, if \tcode{operator+} worked with \tcode{const&},
lots of allocations would be unnecessarily thrown away.

The downside of this design is that every binary operator needs to be a template
accepting forwarding references,
and this possibly applies to other future operations like \tcode{std::gcd} as well.
However, this implementation complexity is not really perceived by the user.
If the operators were not \q{optimal},
the user would need to avoid overhead by writing:
\cppblock{
big_int result = y * z;
result += x;
}

It's obvious that the complexity of managing allocations optimally goes \em{somewhere}.
The question is just whether it should be done invisibly by the standard library,
or whether the burden is off-loaded onto users.
We don't see a compelling reason to off-load onto users.

\h4{Why not return by \tt{&&} from unary operators?}

For unary operators, it would also be possible to return by xvalue instead of by prvalue,
but this would mean that the value category of the result of operators is not always the same.
The perceived benefit is that an unnecessary extra object is avoided.

\Bex{
The hypothetical overload set for \tcode{operator+} looks as follows:
\cppblock{
constexpr basic_big_int   operator+() const&;
constexpr basic_big_int&& operator+() && noexcept;
}
}

It would also mean that users run danger of holding onto objects longer than they expected:
\cppblock{
auto&& x = ~std::move(y);
}
If \tcode{x} refers to the original object \tcode{y},
a data race could be introduced by working with \tcode{x} on one thread,
and overwriting \tcode{y = 0;} on another thread,
each of which is well-defined on its own.

Overall, this idea is making things more complicated and dangerous,
with the only benefit being a micro-optimization
which we don't see as sufficiently important.

\h3{Why not provide lower-level operations for arithmetic?}

A common idea in the committee is that rather than providing the \q{high-level construct}
of \tcode{std::big_int},
we could (first) provide the low-level building blocks,
such as multiprecision operations.
Then, any third-party library could easily build its own \tcode{big_int}
type on top of such operations.

There is some merit to this idea;
our \ref("Reference-Impl") internally dispatches to a number of functions
that operate on \tcode{span<uint_multiprecision_t>},
and those could be exposed directly to the user.
However, we don't propose any of these low-level operations in this paper for a number of reasons:

\ul{
  \li{
    The low-level operations are not actually useful in isolation
    and not actually useful in a wide variety of domains.
    Unlike other numeric operations like those in \header{cmath},
    the multiprecision \tcode{span} operations are basically
    only useful for implementing \tcode{big_int}.
  }
  \li{
    Fixed-width multiprecision operations should already be covered by \tcode{_BitInt(N)},
    making the \tcode{span} arithmetic functions even more limited in scope.
  }
  \li{
    Many of the operations require additional allocations.
    For example, \tcode{operator/} requires additional temporary \tcode{basic_big_int},
    unless the divisor has only one limb.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{big_int} does an enormous amount of work by dispatching to the proper operation
    depending on\ul{
      \li{type of operands (one may be of integral type),}
      \li{value category of operands (to repurpose allocations),}
      \li{limb count (different multiplication algorithms are chosen based on operand size),}
      \li{constant evaluation,}
      \li{
        the availability of \tcode{_BitInt(N)}
        (if a sufficiently large \tcode{_BitInt} is available for the operation,
        the whole operation can be delegated to intrinsics
        by converting both sides to \tcode{_BitInt}),
      }
      \li{\etc}
    }
    Providing the user only with the \tcode{span} operation
    which does not have this vast amount of extra logic
    is not actually in their best interest.
    It would be like providing them with a \tcode{sqrt} function that doesn't handle
    infinities and NaNs and telling them that this \tcode{sqrt} is a lower-level building block.
    In reality, it's simply an incomplete and less useful version of \tcode{std::sqrt}.
    Analogously, in the best case, their \tcode{big_int} operations would end up recreating
    the standard library implementation verbatim.
    In the worst (and much more likely case),
    the user's \tcode{big_int} would end up worse than the standard library version.
  }
  \li{
    The design of these \tcode{span} operations is extremely complicated in itself.
    Among other important questions, it is unclear whether the operations should
    be templates that can take \tcode{span} with both static and dynamic extent,
    how allocators should be passed (if at all), etc.
  }
  \li{
    The motivation of this paper is largely based on
    using \tcode{big_int} as a vocabulary type and as a \q{safe and dynamic} alternative
    to fixed-width integers.
    None of that motivates these \tcode{span} operations.
  }
  \li{
    There is no urgency to add \tcode{span} operations as a prerequisite for \tcode{std::big_int}.
    If those low-level operations were ever added,
    the implementation of \tcode{std::big_int} could simply start using them.
    There is no ABI break waiting to happen by standardizing \tcode{std::big_int}
    without those \tcode{span} operations.
  }
  \li{
    The \tcode{span} operations are a pessimization for constant evaluation.
    At least in theory, it is possible to make \tcode{std::big_int} hold a
    \tcode{std::meta::info} or some other \q{compiler internals handle type}
    which directly references the compiler's arbitrary-precision integer type.
    See also \ref("#big-int-compiler-intrinsic").
    The \q{compiler handle approach}
    can result in much more lightweight constant evaluation,
    and only requires allocating when the object is transitioned from its
    compile-time representation to its runtime representation.
    By contrast, the \tcode{span} operations necessitate
    actually having a \tcode{span<uint_multiprecision_t>} and managing its allocations,
    so more work is done in the frontend.
  }
}

Overall, we don't see a need for these \tcode{span} operations in this paper.
They might be useful in spite of the problems listed above,
but are an entirely separate feature with separate motivation.

The explanation above also reveals that the premise of \tcode{span} operations being \q{lower-level}
is simply wrong, to an extent.
The \tcode{std::big_int} level is the lowest level at which approaches such as
wrapping a handle to \tcode{llvm::APint} is feasible.
The more \tcode{std::big_int} is treated as a compiler intrinsic,
the lower-level it conceptually becomes.

\h3{Template parameters}

\h4{Order of template parameters}

For \tcode{std::basic_big_int},
the order is generally \q{most likely to be specified as an argument first}:

\cppblock{
template<size_t min_inplace_capacity,
         class Limb = uint_multiprecision_t,
         class Allocator = allocator<Limb>>
  class basic_big_int;
}

The \tcode{Allocator} parameter needs to come after the \tcode{Limb} parameter
so that the default argument \tcode{allocator<Limb>} can be spelled.
This is unfortunate because the limb type cannot be configured at all
(it must always be \tcode{uint_multiprecision_t}),
but would not be so bad if it became genuinely configurable in the future.
Fortunately, the only people inconvenienced are power users who use \tcode{std::basic_big_int}
with a custom allocator,
which is acceptable.

\h4{Limb type parameter}

When it comes to allowing configuration of the limb type,
there is a spectrum of solutions:

\ul{
  \li{
    Don't allow any limb type configuration and have no template parameter.
    This is a simple design, but closes the door to future extensions forever
    because adding another template parameter would be an API break.
  }
  \li{
    \mark{\b{Ours:}}
    Have a limb type parameter \tcode{class Limb}
    but only allow \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t} as an argument.
    This enables future extension.
  }
  \li{
    Allow types as arguments that are \q{functionally equivalent} to
    \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t}.
    That is, they are distinct types that have the same object and value representation,
    like \tcode{unsigned long} and \tcode{unsigned long long} on 64-bit Linux.
  }
  \li{
    Allow some subset of unsigned integer types,
    like 32-bit and 64-bit integers.
  }
  \li{
    Allow arbitrary unsigned integer types.
  }
}

While we are not proposing any arguments other than \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t}
in this paper,
such future extension could be very reasonable for a few reasons:
\ul{
  \li{
    Existing code that uses either \tcode{unsigned long} or \tcode{unsigned long long}
    as a limb type would experience friction
    when working with spans of \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t},
    even if those types are functionally equivalent.
    For example, there would be no guarantee that an existing C API yielding a
    \tcode{const unsigned long*} to the user could obtain that pointer
    directly from \tcode{std::big_int::representation()}.
  }
  \li{
    Some configuration of the limb type is reasonably implementable and quite useful.
    For example, 64-bit code interfacing with 32-bit legacy code bases could benefit
    from working with a 32-bit limb type.
  }
  \li{
    Within a few decades, 128-bit arithmetic support may arrive on 64-bit platforms,
    similar to how WASM32 supports 64-bit arithmetic operations
    but has a 32-bit address space.
    If so, there ought to be a way to create a \tcode{std::basic_big_int} with 128-bit limbs,
    even if \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t} cannot be redefined due to ABI concerns.
  }
}

Even if one doesn't find these arguments compelling,
is there strong enough reason to make it impossible to ever extend \tcode{basic_big_int}
with a different limb type?
Probably not.
The template parameter only mildly inconveniences implementers and power users
who don't merely use \tcode{std::big_int}.
Most users work with \tcode{std::string}
rather than \tcode{std::basic_string},
and we expect a similar pattern for \tcode{std::big_int} vs. \tcode{std::basic_big_int}.

\Bnote{
A similar approach was recently taken in \ref("P2019R9") \q{Thread attributes},
in \eelis{thread.thread.class.general}:
\cppblock{
template <same_as<char> T>
  class name_hint;
}
}

\Bnote{
Supporting \q{functionally equivalent} unsigned integer types would ideally be done
with some compiler support or by ignoring certain instances of UB.
The underlying operations working with \tcode{span<uint_multiprecision_t>}
could be \q{blessed} so that internally,
e.g. \tcode{unsigned long} can alias \tcode{unsigned long long} and \tcode{_BitInt(64)}.
This would allow having only one internal function while still handling all types.
}

\h3{Conversions}

\h4{Converting constructor}

We provide the following constructor for implicit conversions:
\cppblock{
template<\exposid{arbitrary-arithmetic-type} T>
  constexpr explicit(\seebelow) basic_big_int(T&& x)
    noexcept(\exposid{no-alloc-constructible-from}<T>);
}

This allows:
\ul{
  \li{
    An implicit conversion from any integer type.
    This makes it possible to write functions such as \tcode{gcd(std::big_int, std::big_int)}
    and call them like \tcode{gcd(x, 3)}.
    The conversion should not be explicit because it would make initialization from literals
    too tedious,
    and the conversion is value-preserving anyway (not counting allocation failure).
  }
  \li{
    An explicit conversion from any floating-point type.
    This could be implicit, but is not value-preserving.
    See also \ref("#explicit-conversions-to-arithmetic-types") for more rationale.
    Similar to division by zero and analogous to converting \tcode{float} to \tcode{int},
    converting non-finite values results in undefined behavior.
    See also \ref("#error-handling").
  }
  \li{
    An explicit conversion from any other specialization of \tcode{std::basic_big_int}.
    This makes it possible to convert between \tcode{std::basic_big_int} with different allocators
    and SOO sizes,
    and make it possible to convert \tcode{std::big_int} literals like \tcode{123n}
    to other specializations.
    Similar constructors
    like the \tcode{\exposid{string-view-like}} constructor of \tcode{basic_string}
    are traditionally also \tcode{explicit}.
  }
}

The \tcode{\exposid{no-alloc-constructible-from}<T>} part ensures
that the constructor is \tcode{noexcept} if no allocation
ever needs to take place to represent the value.

\Bex{
Consider converting \tcode{std::int32_t} to \tcode{basic_big_int<32>}.
The \tcode{inplace_capacity} is at least 32 bits,
so any \tcode{int32_t} can be represented without allocation,
and the converting constructor is \tcode{noexcept}.
}

Since floating-point types may have non-finite values and converting those to \tcode{std::big_int}
results in undefined behavior,
conversions from floating-point types are never \tcode{noexcept},
consistent with the Lakos rule.

\h4{Explicit conversions to arithmetic types}

\tcode{std::big_int} is generally designed to mirror the interface of fundamental integer types,
though conversions are a notable exception.
For example, any integer type can be implicitly converted to \tcode{int},
but \tcode{std::big_int} makes this \tcode{explicit}.
This follows the general design direction of more explicit conversions in new C++ types,
like \tcode{std::float64_t} → \tcode{std::float32_t},
non-value-preserving conversions in \header{simd}, etc.

Besides following an established design direction,
we also consider it surprising if one could convert a dynamically sized \tcode{std::big_int}
which potentially holds thousands of bits to an \tcode{int},
without ever expressing that intent in code.
There would also be no way to catch such conversions with list-initialization
since user-defined conversions are never narrowing.

\h4{Bit-precise integer interoperability}

While \ref("P3666R4") has not been merged into C++29 yet,
we still need to consider how \tcode{std::big_int} will interoperate with \tcode{_BitInt}.
Notably, the constructor of \tcode{std::big_int} may or may not reject those integers,
and the user-defined conversion to integer types may or may not be valid.

We believe that this conversion should be valid in both directions because it exposes a
fundamental and clearly useful bit of functionality.
There is simply no good reason to prevent these conversions,
other than wanting to prevent \tcode{_BitInt} from ever being a useful and fully supported
type in the standard library.

\Bex{
One use case where \tcode{_BitInt} is specifically useful is for providing large integer literals.
For example, \tcode{std::big_int{123wb}} can be used instead of \tcode{123n},
which bypasses the user-defined \tcode{operator""n} literal,
and that UDL's implementation is relatively complicated and heavy.
As long as a sufficiently wide \tcode{_BitInt} type exists,
its \gterm{integer-literal} is a lighter-weight alternative.
}

\Bex{
Another use case is a cryptography library
that uses \tcode{std::big_int} at its library boundaries,
but performs the underlying calculations (such as for RSA) using e.g. \tcode{_BitInt(8192)}.
This clearly requires converting \tcode{std::big_int} to \tcode{_BitInt(8192)},
and a user would need to perform that conversion manually limb-by-limb
if the standard library didn't provide it.

Such a conversion is also clearly useful when handing the values of \tcode{std::big_int}
off to a C library that uses \tcode{_BitInt(128)}, \tcode{_BitInt(256)}, etc. in its API.
}

\h3{User-defined literals for \tt{std::big_int}}

For convenient creation of \tcode{std::big_int},
we provide the following user-defined literal:

\cppblock{
inline namespace literals {
  inline namespace big_int_literals {
    template<char... digits> constexpr big_int operator""n() noexcept(\seebelow);
    template<char... digits> constexpr big_int operator""N() noexcept(\seebelow);
  }
}
}

The \tcode{inline namespace} approach is the same as for other standard library UDLs,
such as \tcode{std::literals::string_\cowel_to_html{\'SHY'}view_literals::operator""sv}.

\Bex{
The user could write the following code:
\cppblock{
using namespace std::big_int_literals;
auto x = 123n;
}
}

\h4(id="udl-suffix-naming"){Why \tt{123n}?}

The suffix '\tt{n}' was chosen for symmetry with JavaScript,
where \tcode{123n} is equivalent to \tcode{BigInt(123)}.

\h4{User-defined literals vs. string constructors}

It is worth noting that other libraries typically don't provide a UDL.
For example, Boost.Multiprecision instead has a string constructor,
expecting users to write \tcode{boost::cpp_int("123")}.
We opted not to match that design because
\ul{
  \li{
    The \tcode{123n} syntax is more natural and concise than \tcode{std::big_int("123")}.
  }
  \li{
    The \tcode{"123"} syntax pessimizes runtime performance.
    The UDL approach allows pre-computing a \tcode{constexpr uint_multiprecision_t[]}
    holding the limbs during constant evaluation.
    The runtime call to \tcode{operator""()} only needs to copy the pre-computed limb array.
    Such a design is only possible thanks to \tcode{template<char...>}
    giving us the literal as a constant expression.
  }
  \li{
    Third-party libraries avoid this form of UDL in part because it is very complicated
    to implement and requires parsing the integer literal with all features
    (including base prefixes, digit separators, etc.).
    In the standard, we can consider this to be an insignificant QoI issue.
    In the standard library implementation,
    we also have the luxury of doing the UDL parsing using a compiler intrinsic
    if it turns out to be too expensive to do in constant evaluation.
    Third-party libraries don't have this luxury.
  }
}

\Bnote{
As mentioned in \ref("#big-int-compiler-intrinsic"),
a future direction may be to extend the UDL syntax to support multiprecision, like:
\cppblock{
constexpr big_int operator""n(const uint_multiprecision_t* limbs, size_t size);
}

Here, the compiler would do the work of parsing the literal and turning it into a limb array.
This would avoid turning \tcode{operator""n} into a template.
}

While this new form of UDL is not proposed here,
it still informs us that there is potential to improve UDLs and reduce some of their costs,
without breaking existing uses of \tcode{123n}.
This makes UDLs a more compelling option right now.

\h4{Why provide UDLs or string constructors at all?}

\em{Some} form of convenient construction is necessary.
Otherwise, creating large constant \tcode{std::big_int} objects is simply too tedious.

It would also be possible using the constructor
taking a \tcode{span<const uint_multiprecision_t>},
but this would require breaking any large constants up into individual limbs,
which is very difficult to do portably
considering that \tcode{uint_multiprecision_t} varies in size.

Another option is to rely on \tcode{_BitInt},
but this feature is not yet available in the C++ standard,
and even if it was,
the \tcode{BITINT_MAXWIDTH} is only guaranteed to be at least \tcode{LLONG_WIDTH}.
In other words, \tcode{_BitInt}s larger than 64-bit are an optional feature.

\h3{Adjacent library utilities}

\h4{Hash support for \tt{std::big_int}}

We also provide \tt{std::hash} support.
While the hashing algorithm is up to the implementation
(there are various widely used algorithms for hashing a sequence of integers),
there is an important restriction:
for any \tcode{basic_big_int<b, A>} object,
the \tcode{std::hash} result depends solely on the integer value
(i.e. the mathematical value represented) by the object.

This makes it possible to use a different \tcode{basic_big_int} as a key type
than the type used for lookup (assuming transparent lookup in some container).

However, the hash might not be compatible with that of fundamental types,
i.e. \tcode{1} may be hashed differently than \tcode{std::big_int{1}}.
This was done to increase flexibility in the \tcode{hash<big_int>} implementation
and to not pessimize the hash algorithm.

\Bdecision{
We should seriously consider whether we want hash parity between fundamental types
and \tcode{basic_big_int}.
}

\h4{\tt{std::numeric_limits} specialization}

There should be a specialization of \tcode{std::numeric_limits}
provided for \tcode{std::basic_big_int}.
Doing so is tradition in third-party libraries that provide their own infinite-precision integers,
and it provides useful capabilities such as writing generic code that works with
\tcode{int}, \tcode{big_int}, and \tcode{boost::cpp_int},
and distinguishing the infinite-precision integers via
\tcode{numeric_limits::is_bounded}.

\h4{\tt{std::abs}}

We provide a \tcode{std::abs} overload for \tcode{std::big_int} as follows:

\cppblock{
template<class T> constexpr remove_cvref_t<T> abs(T&& x)
  noexcept(noexcept(-std::forward<T>(x)));
}

\'HORIZONTAL ELLIPSIS' where \tcode{remove_cvref_t<T>}
is any specialization of \tcode{basic_big_int}.

This is an obviously useful overload that any user would expect to exist.

Header management provides a problem:
we don't want \header{cstdlib} and \header{cmath} to include \header{big_int},
and both of these provide the overloads for other integer types.
To solve this, \header{big_int} provides the overload
as well as the rest of the overload set
(not providing the rest of the overload set would risk ODR violations).

\Bnote{
There is still \em{some} risk of ODR violations if \tcode{std::big_int}
leaks through some other header,
the user included only \header{cstdlib} and
their call to \tcode{std::abs} with \tcode{std::big_int} fails,
but it's unclear how this problem could arise in practice.

Both \header{numeric} and \header{charconv} properly include \header{big_int},
so the user gets all the associated functionality.
}

\Bnote{
The overload expects a forwarding reference
because when given an rvalue of type \tcode{basic_big_int},
allocations can be avoided;
\tcode{std::abs} simply flips the sign bit of the object,
enabled by the \ref("#sign-and-magnitude") representation.
This is also why the \tcode{noexcept} requirement is delegated to \tcode{operator-}.
By contrast, \tcode{std::abs} on lvalues may require allocation.
}

\h4{\tt{std::saturating_cast}}

We provide a \tcode{std::saturating_cast} overload for \tcode{std::big_int} as follows:

\cppblock{
template<class R, size_t b, class L, class A>
  constexpr R saturating_cast(const basic_big_int<b, L, A>& x) noexcept;
}

\'HORIZONTAL ELLIPSIS' where \tcode{R} is a signed or unsigned integer type.

This is partially motivated by the fact that Boost's
\tcode{static_cast} for \tcode{cpp_int} behaves in a saturating
instead of truncating way.
While that behavior is useful,
it would be surprising to C++ users who expect conversions to truncate,
and it would be inconvenient in generic code that tries to handle
both \tcode{std::big_int} and fundamental integer types.
In any case, providing \tcode{std::saturating_cast} provides an easy \q{upgrade path}
from \tcode{boost::cpp_int} to \tcode{std::big_int}.

\h3{Naming}

The name \tcode{std::big_int} was chosen because it meets user expectations
and makes its design instantly clear.
In languages where infinite-precision integers are not built-in,
the name is virtually always some variant of \q{big int} or \q{big integer}.
The name \tcode{std::big_int} is also immediately recognizable
as supporting infinite-precision arithmetic and as growing elastically as needed.

Previous proposals used the name \tcode{std::integer},
but this name doesn't convey the design well,
and is too similar to concept names like \tcode{std::integral}.

\h3{Choice of header}

We propose a new \header{big_int} header that just provides \tcode{std::big_int}
and its utility functions.

While \tcode{std::big_int} could belong in \header{numeric},
that header is already enormous,
and \tcode{std::big_int} is essentially a standalone container.
It could be seen as a \tcode{std::vector} with some operator overloads.
Containers traditionally have their own headers, like \header{vector}.

\h3{Error handling}

\tcode{std::big_int} has elastic operations,
meaning that it grows as needed to fit the result,
making many operations infallible.
The allocations can still fail and throw \tcode{std::bad_alloc},
same as for any other container.

Fallible operations include
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{operator/} and \tcode{operator%},
    if the divisor is zero,
  }
  \li{
    conversion from \tcode{numeric_limits<float>::infinity()}
    to \tcode{std::big_int}, and
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{operator<<} and \tcode{operator>>} with negative shifts.
  }
}

We handle this as undefined behavior.
From a runtime cost perspective,
this is unjustified because \tcode{operator/} already cannot be minimal and entirely branchless
due to the \ref("#small-object-optimizations"),
even if there is no extra check for division by zero.
\tcode{std::big_int} division is also a fairly heavy operation,
so the general principle of \q{don't harden high-performance, low-level numerics}
doesn't really apply here.

The reason we still have undefined behavior for these cases is that there is no clear precedent
for error handling in \q{heavy numerics} in the standard library.
\tcode{std::bitset} offers some precedent,
but arguably goes too far by throwing on runtime checks even in fast and low-level operations
like \tcode{operator[]},
making the type less attractive for some high-performance use cases.
The proposed undefined behavior can be thought of as a placeholder,
and undefined behavior can always be \q{filled in} with something better at a later point.

\:::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::

\h2{Future direction}

The initial \tcode{std::big_int} proposal is only the start.
There are many features that could be added subsequently
but which are not in this paper to keep its scope focused.

\h3{Integer numeric functions}

This paper only provides \tcode{std::big_int} overloads for pre-existing functions,
such as \tcode{std::abs}.
However, infinite-precision libraries typically provide many more operations, such as
\ul{
  \li{modular arithmetic functions, like \tcode{powmod},}
  \li{integer exponentiation (\tcode{ipow}),}
  \li{integer logarithm (\tcode{ilog}),}
  \li{integer square root (\tcode{isqrt}),}
  \li{factorial (\tcode{fact}),}
  \li{binomial coefficient (\tcode{choose}),}
  \li{\etc}
}

The key observation is that these functions are at least as useful for fundamental integer types,
not just for \tcode{std::big_int},
and they should be designed for integers in general.
That makes all of them separate and orthogonal features,
where a \tcode{std::big_int} overload is just one of the design aspects.

\Bnote{
The \code("ts"){bigint} type in JavaScript was standardized in such a minimal form as well:
only the numeric type itself is provided,
while the additional \tcode("ts"){Math} utilities are largely missing.
}

\h3{Fixed-point interoperability}

To provide interoperability with fixed-point numbers,
a conversion to/from such numbers could be provided.
Concretely, this would require
\ul{
  \li{
    a constructor taking the integer and its scale (in bits),
  }
  \li{
    a conversion function that yields the integer and its scale.
  }
}

Neither is truly necessary because
conversion from fixed-point could also be implemented in terms of
construction from an integer followed by \tcode{<<=} or \tcode{>>=}.
conversion to fixed-point could also be implemented in terms of a
\tcode{std::countl_zero} overload for \tcode{std::big_int} and in terms of \tcode{>>}.
However, more direct implementations may provide some convenience.

In any case,
there is no urgent need to provide such convenience in this proposal,
especially considering that there are zero-overhead workarounds using bit-shifting.

\h3{Random number generation}

Adding support for \tcode{std::big_int} in \header{random} is another logical next step,
but out of scope in this paper in order to limit the scope.
Presumably, the approach would be to allow \tcode{std::big_int} in
\tcode{std::uniform_int_distribution} and other facilities.

\h3{Making \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t} C-compatible}

As mentioned in \ref("#c-compatibility"),
it may be beneficial to improve C compatibility
by putting \tcode{std::uint_multiprecision_t} in \header{cstdint},
and possibly by standardizing the type for C2y.

\:::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::

\h2{Arguments against standardization}

While there is strong motivation to include \tcode{std::big_int} in the standard,
there are also some arguments against doing so, which need to be considered carefully.

\h3{Many trade-offs}

\tcode{std::big_int} is relatively complex,
and any complex design comes with a number of trade-offs.
This does provide some motivation against standardization.
Generally speaking, if a feature has so many trade-offs that most
users will have to make their own form of a feature,
this suggests that the feature is unfit for standardization.

Some concrete trade-offs for \tcode{std::big_int} (and our response to these) are:

\style{
.row-seps td {
  border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color);
}
}

\table(class="row-seps"){
  \tr{
    \th{Trade-off}
    \th{Explanation}
    \th{Our response}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{SOO}
    \td{
      We force \ref("#small-object-optimizations"),
      which does pessimize the use case where the values are always huge.
    }
    \td{
      \ul{
        \li{
          Providing a single \tcode{std::big_int} non-template alias
          prevents a pessimization of the interface
          where the user would always have to provide the \tcode{small_capacity} and where
          there would be no true vocabulary type.
        }
        \li{
          In the use case where \tcode{std::big_int} always stores huge values,
          the cost of allocation and arithmetic dominates the small added cost of increased
          container size and branching.
        }
      }
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{SOO size}
    \td{
      Depending on the use case,
      different \b{amounts of small object optimization} are appropriate.
    }
    \td{
      We solve this by making the amount configurable like in \tcode{std::basic_big_int<128>}.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Allocation}
    \td{
      Different use cases require different \b{forms of allocation}.
    }
    \td{
      We address this by making \tcode{std::big_int} allocator-aware.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Constant-time operations}
    \td{
      In certain applications such as in cryptography,
      \b{constant-time operations} are desired, and \tcode{std::big_int}
      is not designed around constant execution time.
    }
    \td{
      \ul{
        \li{
          Constant-time execution requires constant integer sizes,
          so this use case is actually covered by \tcode{_BitInt}, not \tcode{std::big_int}.
          It's fundamentally implausible for 1-bit integer operations to have the same cost
          as 1024-bit integer operations,
          no matter what design trade-offs are made.
          In other words, there is no trade-off because
          \tcode{std::big_int} would not be used for this purpose anyway.
        }
        \li{
          \tcode{std::big_int} is still useful as a vocabulary type
          at cryptographic library boundaries.
          For example, Java's BouncyCastle cryptographic library uses \tcode{BigInteger}
          in its API,
          but the underlying cryptographic algorithms may operate on fixed-width arrays of integers.
          See also \ref("#std::big_int-is-a-vocabulary-type").
        }
      }
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Representation}
    \td{
      The \b{representation} could be \ref("#sign-and-magnitude"), two's complement,
      or anything else.
      The choice depends on what is most convenient for the surrounding code.
    }
    \td{
      In theory, any representation could be chosen,
      but for reasons explained in the referenced section,
      this is almost always sign and magnitude.
      The choice largely boils down to minimizing the cost of operations.
      Two's complement types like \tcode{llvm::APInt} are a rare exception.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Elasticity}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::big_int} operations are \b{elastic}
      (meaning growth happens automatically to fit the result),
      but some use cases require \b{inelastic} results,
      like \tcode{llvm::APInt}.
    }
    \td{
      This is arguably not a trade-off because inelastic operations can always be added later,
      via e.g. \tcode{std::fixed_add} or \tcode{std::wrapping_add} functions.
      The trade-off is limited to the fact that those operations would not simply be spelled
      \tcode{operator+}.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Error handling}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::big_int} is entirely non-throwing,
      except for exceptions thrown during allocation failure
      or as the result of undefined behavior
      (e.g. division by zero in \tcode{operator/}).
    }
    \td{
      \ul{
        \li{
          Making the type mostly non-throwing is the most flexible choice
          and even caters to freestanding use cases.
          Due to operations being elastic,
          almost none of them require error handling anyway,
          except for allocation,
          the error handling of which can be customized.
        }
        \li{
          The undefined behavior in the rare error cases
          does not preclude anyone from using the type.
          They can always add their own runtime checks where needed to prevent UB.
          It would be much less flexible if the runtime check was forced
          and came with a certain behavior, like throwing an exception.
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

In summary, the idea that \tcode{std::big_int} has too many domain-specific trade-offs
is largely unsubstantiated.
Allowing configuration of the \tcode{inplace_capacity} and allocator
provides sufficient flexibility for virtually every use case.
Any remaining trade-off in the design of \tcode{std::big_int} would be mildly annoying at worst.

\Bnote{
That does not mean that no one will ever have to make their own \tcode{std::big_int} type;
there will always be people creating their own \tcode{std::vector}, their own \tcode{std::sort},
etc. because their use is simply too specialized for the standard library.
Some people even make their own standard library.
However, our \tcode{std::big_int} is \q{good enough to be everyone's default},
which is good enough for any standard feature.
}

Another way to look at this issue is to consider whether
\tcode{std::big_int} is suitable for each of the advertised \ref("#use-cases")
(serialization, safety, scripting engines, statistics, scientific computing, and cryptography).
For each of these, it is possible to imagine a reason to avoid \tcode{std::big_int},
like not being optimized with manual assembly for some architecture used in scientific computing,
but \tcode{std::big_int} remains an acceptable default choice for most users;
there is no \q{deal breaker} among the trade-offs above
that would make it unsuitable for the domain.

\h3{Use of committee resources}

Another concern with \tcode{std::big_int} is that it undoubtedly consumes
committee resources both to standardize initially,
and to maintain.
This is naturally the case with any large feature.

However, big integer types have many millions of uses throughout
various programming languages already,
and standardization will likely make that number skyrocket for C++,
so this is simply committee time well-spent,
however much time it is.

\h3{Increased complexity of future integer features}

The current design approach of \tcode{std::big_int} is to closely imitate
the fundamental integer types.
Rather than providing operations as member functions,
existing standard library facilities like \tcode{std::gcd} and \tcode{std::popcount}
should be extended to support \tcode{std::big_int}
in addition to signed and/or unsigned integer types.
Consequently, once a \q{decent amount} of coverage for \tcode{std::big_int} is provided,
the expectation is that any new integer utility would also support \tcode{std::big_int}.
This is very similar to how any new functions in \header{cmath} or \header{bit}
are now expected to have \tcode{std::simd} overloads,
for the sake of consistency.

We don't consider this permanent increase in standardization effort
to detract from \tcode{std::big_int} because
\ul{
  \li{
    In a lot of cases,
    those new integer utilities are a useful part of big integer libraries anyway.
    For example, those libraries typically come with integer exponentiation functions,
    so \tcode{std::big_int} support for such a new \tcode{std::ipow} function
    would be added enthusiastically, not reluctantly.
    See also \ref("#integer-numeric-functions").
  }
  \li{
    It is quite easy to design the function signatures for new \tcode{std::big_int}
    utilities once there are some examples in the standard library setting a precedent.
  }
  \li{
    A naive implementation is often quite easy to provide for \tcode{std::big_int},
    by simply doing the same as for the fixed-width integers.
    This is very similar to how the naive implementation for \tcode{simd::vec} is to perform
    the scalar operation elementwise.
  }
}

In short, the added cost to new integer utilities is either low or effectively zero
(if we would have added \tcode{std::big_int} support anyway,
it's not really an additional cost).

\h3{Use of implementer resources}

Another similar criticism is that \tcode{std::big_int} would consume a substantial amount
of implementer resources,
especially when the goal is to have a high-performance implementation.

There are many factors that address this issue:
\ul{
  \li{
    Standardization of the type concentrates the work of third-party libraries in
    the standard library.
    Many upgrades to e.g. Boost.Multiprecision might also be \q{backported}
    to the standard library counterpart, and vice versa.
  }
  \li{
    Due to how ridiculously widespread big integers are in C++ already
    (see GitHub code search in \ref("#introduction")),
    standardization would act as an invite to outside collaborators.
  }
  \li{
    Every major compiler vendor already provides a big integer type,
    such as \tcode{llvm::APInt}.
    While some major refactoring would be required,
    vendors could end up converting these types into wrappers around \tcode{std::big_int}
    or could at least implement the numeric operations via \tcode{std::big_int}.
    For example, \tcode{llvm::APIntOps::pext} is the spiritual equivalent
    of a \tcode{std::bit_compress} overload for \tcode{std::big_int},
    so why maintain both?
  }
}

The biggest remaining concern is that standardization trifurcates the implementations into
a libstdc++ \tcode{std::big_int},
a libc++ \tcode{std::big_int}, and
an MSVC \tcode{std::big_int}.
However, this work is already trifurcated among the vendors' existing big integer types (see above),
so standardization does not create additional duplicated effort
so much as relocate this existing work for the benefit of users.

\h3{Residual fragmentation}

A possible criticism of \tcode{std::big_int} is that
\q{everyone already has their own big integer}.
Or more precisely, the ecosystem is already full of solutions
such as GMP, Boost.Multiprecision, etc. or various internal implementations
of multiprecision used in cryptographic libraries.
Even if \tcode{std::big_int} was standardized,
the ecosystem wouldn't simply drop their own solutions overnight.

While that is true,
these arguments shouldn't stop any proposal on their own.
Any new feature takes time to adopt,
and even if it provides a better or more convenient solution to a problem,
it's unrealistic for everyone to rewrite their existing solutions.
Similarly, the addition of C++17 \tcode{std::string_view}
does not mean that everyone would instantly move away
from existing solutions like \tcode{gsl::string_view},
but that doesn't make C++17's new type worthless.

\h4{N-to-N conversion problem}

Furthermore, there is an N-to-N conversion problem between all these existing solutions.
How does one convert \tcode{boost::cpp_int} to e.g. OpenSSL's \tcode{BIGNUM}?
The most universal form of conversion is to convert to a string and parse as another type,
but this is comically expensive and involves multiprecision division operations.

\tcode{std::big_int} provides at least an intermediate representation that turns this into a 1-to-N
conversion problem.
That is, any existing big integer type can be extended with a constructor taking
\tcode{const std::big_int&} or \tcode{std::span<const std::uint_multiprecision_t>}
to facilitate conversions,
and any library with some existing internal integer type can at least provide new functions
taking such parameters in its top-level API.

\h3{Inability to change ABI}

Since the standard library is typically kept in an ABI-stable state,
standardizing \tcode{std::big_int} has the downside of losing some potential flexibility
compared to third-party libraries.
A crucial question is thus whether frequent changes to the representation
of \tcode{std::big_int} are plausible and desirable,
i.e. whether we forfeit much in this regard.

We believe this is either not the case or can be mitigated with our design,
for the following reasons:
\ul{
  \li{
    The SOO size can be customized with the \tcode{min_inplace_capacity} parameter,
    so even if a user realizes that the \tcode{std::big_int} layout is not suitable for them,
    they can choose another \tcode{std::basic_big_int}.
  }
  \li{
    The overall sign-and-magnitude layout is an industry standard that has not seen
    meaningful change in the last decades.
    Most big integer implementations use this layout.
  }
  \li{
    Even more fundamentally,
    the approach of performing multi-precision operations using limb arrays is an eternal standard.
    For over 50 years, CPU instructions like \tt{adc} have been specifically designed
    around performing multi-precision operations word-by-word,
    and arranging words in an array has always been the obvious layout to make this seamless.
  }
  \li{
    The layout of Boost.Multiprecision's \tcode{boost::cpp_int} has seen little change historically.
  }
  \li{
    For really large integers (multi-limb case),
    the underlying operations work with \tcode{span<uint_multiprecision_t>},
    and the details of how those limbs are laid out in the container are somewhat irrelevant.
  }
}

In conclusion,
while the \q{ABI freeze} is a real concern with \tcode{std::big_int},
the ABI is fairly stable in practice,
for various innate reasons.
However, it is plausible that some details of how \ref("#small-object-optimizations")
are implemented may be imperfect at first,
and tweaking those details would be difficult in the standard library.

\h3{Header bloat}

The design strategy for \tcode{std::big_int} is to provide supporting utilities as overloads
of existing standard library functions.
That is, \tcode{std::to_chars}, \tcode{std::gcd}, etc.
receive new overloads accepting \tcode{std::big_int}.
An unfortunate consequence is that this bloats any and all integer utilities with
\tcode{std::big_int} support,
including formerly lightweight headers like \header{charconv}.

To be fair:
\ul{
  \li{
    We have never really kept our headers minimal,
    with some extreme example like including \header{memory} for \tcode{std::to_address}
    and getting all of \tcode{std::atomic}, \tcode{std::shared_ptr},
    and \tcode{uninitialized_*} algorithms.
  }
  \li{
    None of this is a problem with \tcode{import std;}.
  }
  \li{
    \header{big_int} is still relatively lightweight.
    It manages its representation similar to \tcode{std::vector},
    and the really complicated numeric parts like Toom-Cook multiplications
    live in the runtime library anyway.
    The \tcode{constexpr} paths use a much smaller implementation.
  }
}

\:::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::

\h2{Implementation experience}

Our reference implementation can be found at \ref("Reference-Impl").

\h3{Deployment experience}

This implementation is still very recent and has no real-world deployment experience.

\h3{Benchmarks}

See \url{https://eisenwave.github.io/std-big-int/benchmarks.html}.
Overall, our \tcode{std::big_int} implementation is typically a bit slower than
\tcode{gmp_int} (Boost wrapper around GMP) and on par with \tcode{boost::cpp_int}
on identical generic driver code
(that is, templates that perform computation for any of these integer types).
These benchmarks are unsurprising:
GMP uses hand-written assembly optimized over many years and
represents somewhat of a theoretical optimum,
and our top-level API and implementation internals are so similar to those of Boost,
not much should be gained or lost in terms of performance.

